.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1214 PM PDT Sun Sep 27 2026
(This evening through Monday night)

High clouds continue to float over the Bay Area and Central Coast,
while stratus coverage lingers across coastal Marin and San Mateo
counties, the Oakland area, the city of San Francisco, and the
southern Monterey Bay region. Some mixing out is possible through
the afternoon where the high clouds clear out, but some coastal
regions may remain socked in through the day. Stratus should return
inland this evening and overnight.

Water vapor imagery shows a deep trough, perhaps even a cut-off low,
centered just off of Cape Mendocino, which brings in cooler air to
the region. Highs today will range in the 70s for the inland
valleys, into the lower to middle 80s for the warmest spots of
southern Monterey County, and the upper 50s to the lower 70s for the
coastal and Bayshore regions. Breezy onshore winds will develop in
the afternoon and evening with gusts of 20 to 30 mph at the coast,
through favored gaps and passes, and within the Salinas Valley.

As the trough/low moves across the state, and ridging comes in
behind it, the upper level winds over the Bay Area and Central Coast
will shift from southwesterly to northeasterly, leading to a push of
offshore flow on Monday morning. The strongest winds look to develop
on the lee sides (southwest-facing slopes) of the interior mountains
of the North Bay, where hydraulic jump effects will push wind gusts
to around 20 mph. High temperatures will warm a fair bit in the
inland valleys, where the forecast calls for middle 70s to middle
80s, and the Bayside locations, which should expect highs in the
upper 60s to middle 70s.

