.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1214 PM PDT Sun Sep 27 2026
(Tuesday through next Saturday)

Additional pushes of higher elevation offshore flow will set up on
Tuesday morning and Wednesday morning, chiefly across the North and
East Bay interior mountains. The strongest push looks to be on
Tuesday morning with wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph possible in the
interior mountains of the North Bay. The potential for wind gusts
above 35 mph is greatest at the ridgeline of the Vaca Mountains
along the eastern border of Napa County.

Otherwise, seasonably warm temperatures should be fully
established by Tuesday as highs in the inland valleys reach the
middle 80s to the middle 90s, those in the Bayshore regions reach
the middle 70s to the middle 80s, and the Pacific coast reaches
the middle 60s to middle 70s. Temperatures may fluctuate by a few
degrees each day but the general trend should remain similar
through the end of the seven day outlook. Some areas of Moderate
HeatRisk, a level of heat that brings a moderate risk of heat-
related illnesses to sensitive populations, will develop through
the next few days, but confidence in widespread impacts is not
high enough to issue a Heat Advisory at this point. Longer-range
guidance suggests that temperatures above the seasonal average
will continue into the middle of October.

As a final point, minor coastal flooding is possible in low-lying
areas across the San Francisco, San Pablo, and Monterey Bay
shorelines Tuesday through Thursday. Up to 1.1 feet of inundation
above normally dry land is expected, including up to 0.6 ft of
surge caused by thermal expansion and sea level rise since the sea
level datum (basically the point where we define as "sea level")
was set in the 1983-2011 period. At the San Francisco tidal gauge,
high tide is expected to be 1.0 ft above normal (6.81 ft MLLW) at
1:30 PM Tuesday 9/29, 1.1 ft above normal (6.93 ft MLLW) at 2:10
PM Wednesday 9/30, and 1.0 ft above normal (6.82 ft MLLW) at 2:58
PM on Thursday 10/1.

