.FIRE WEATHER...
Issued at 1214 PM PDT Sun Sep 27 2026

Today is the last day of seasonally cool and moist conditions before
warmer and drier air comes in for the next week, especially above
the marine layer inversion. Upper level northeasterly flow will
combine with hydraulic jump effects in the lee side of the mountains
to create some breezy offshore gusts each morning from Monday
through Wednesday. The strongest winds are expected on Tuesday
morning in the interior mountains of the North and East Bays, where
wind gusts could reach 30 mph. These offshore winds should
contribute to poor overnight humidity recoveries, with overnight
relative humidities as low as 20-40% above the marine layer, and a
chance for nocturnal drying events across the higher elevations.

